# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# Cached training corpora (cram-corpus). Derived artifacts: reproducible from
# the seed recorded in the sibling .manifest, and large enough that committing
# them would dwarf the source. What's pinned is the generator and its seed —
# same rule as docs/generated/, opposite conclusion about storing the bytes.
/corpora

# Trained checkpoints and their loss curves (`cargo xtask cram`). Same rule:
# derived from a corpus, a config and a seed, all of which are pinned. A model
# is also the worst thing to keep in git — many MB of f32 that re-diffs in full
# on every run.
#
# One exception, force-added rather than un-ignored so the directory stays a
# scratch space: `drivel-b9b10-30k.kvetch` + `.vocab`. Once a checkpoint is embedded
# in a program the kernel boots it stops being a by-product and becomes a contract
# — a fresh clone must build the same guest, and `kvetch-serve`'s `real_checkpoint`
# tests plus the `kvetch-drivel` / `stitch-drivel` itests all run against exactly
# these bytes. (They assert *properties* — the pair decodes and agrees, a completion
# is produced, it leaves the buffer parseable — not an exact byte sequence. The
# byte-exact checksum belongs to the `kvetch-babble` scenario, which recomputes the
# expected completion on the host.) Both halves travel together: a checkpoint
# without its vocab is not a fixture, it is a mispairing waiting to happen (the
# vocab fingerprint in the checkpoint header is what catches that). Replace them
# rarely and in one commit; each version costs 4.2 MB of history forever.
# See plans/kvetch-drivel-on-target.md.
/checkpoints

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/


# Added by cargo

/target

# Integration-test mutex lock. PID-stamped; cleaned up by Drop /
# OS handle close. See itest-harness::lock + xtask/src/itest.rs.
# Unanchored — running xtask itest from a non-root cwd creates the
# lock relative to that cwd; we want all of them ignored.
.itest.lock

# Pending partial baseline from an interrupted itest run.
# Promote with `cargo xtask itest --promote-pending` or discard with
# `--discard-pending`. Same anywhere-cwd reasoning as above.
.itest-baseline.toml.pending

# Per-run history directories (tier 2 NDJSON + tier 3 log copies).
# One subdir per itest invocation. Pruned manually or by
# `cargo xtask itest --prune-runs`. Explicit `**/` makes the
# at-any-depth match unambiguous (a trailing-slash-only pattern
# anchored unexpectedly here on git 2.50).
.itest-runs

# Per-scenario instret cache the snemu-itest audit rewrites every run to
# drive LPT packing. A machine artifact (regenerates on the next run), not a
# reviewed baseline — otherwise it dirties nearly every commit.
.snemu-itest-durations

# Locally-rendered diagram SVGs (`cargo xtask diagram <target>` writes these
# next to the committed mermaid .md). Graphviz layout is version-dependent, so
# they're a local-dev convenience, not a reviewable artifact. The .md is the
# committed source of truth.
docs/generated/*.svg
docs/*.svg
docs/*.png

# Built executables injected into the fs image at build time (kernel/build.rs
# copies them here for SpawnImage). Source files in fs-image/ are committed; the
# generated ELFs under fs-image/bin/ are not.
fs-image/bin/

# RISC-V Image emitted by `cargo xtask image` for the VisionFive 2 (`booti`).
# A build artifact — regenerate from the vf2 kernel; not reviewed.
/snitchos.img
